<h2>The statutory anchor</h2>
<p>The Contempt of Courts Act, 1971 divides contempt into civil and criminal. Section 2(b) defines civil contempt as wilful disobedience of any judgment, decree, direction, order, writ or other process of a court, <em>or wilful breach of an undertaking given to a court</em>. Parliament placed a broken undertaking on the same footing as a disobeyed order: in both cases the court has ordered its affairs — adjourned a hearing, declined an injunction, disposed of a case — on the strength of an assurance that turned out to be hollow.</p>
<p>Section 12 supplies the consequence. Civil contempt is punishable with simple imprisonment for a term up to six months, or fine up to Rs 2,000, or both. The provision also permits the court to discharge the contemnor or remit the punishment where an apology is made to its satisfaction, provided it is bona fide. Courts treat imprisonment as a last resort, but the exposure is real and personal: it attaches to the person who gave the undertaking.</p>

<h2>What counts as an undertaking</h2>
<p>Not everything said in a courtroom binds as an undertaking. Broadly, three forms recur in practice:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Express statement to the court</h4><p>The party, present in person or through an affidavit, states that they will do or refrain from doing something, and the court records the statement in its order and acts upon it.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Counsel's statement on instructions</h4><p>An advocate, on instructions, gives an assurance on the client's behalf which the court records. Such a statement can bind the client, which is why instructions should be specific and preferably written.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Undertakings in recorded settlements</h4><p>A settlement deed or compromise may contain clauses expressed as undertakings to the court, which the court then records in its order while disposing of the matter.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The common thread is that the assurance is directed to the court and the court acts on it. An undertaking recorded in the order is the cleanest case. Where the order is silent, the question becomes one of construction: was the court invited to rely on the promise, and did it do so? Because that inquiry is fact-sensitive, careful practitioners insist that any undertaking be expressly recorded, in precise words, in the order itself.</p>

<h2>Undertaking versus consent term</h2>
<p>The most litigated distinction in this area is between an undertaking given to the court and a term of a compromise agreed between the parties. The two look similar on paper but travel very different enforcement routes.</p>
<table class="law">
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Consent / compromise term</th><th>Undertaking to the court</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>To whom is the obligation owed?</td><td>The opposite party</td><td>The court itself</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Remedy on breach</td><td>Execution of the decree or order embodying the term</td><td>Civil contempt proceedings under the 1971 Act</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mental element</td><td>Breach is enough for execution</td><td>Breach must be wilful</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Consequence</td><td>Enforcement of the obligation itself</td><td>Punishment of the person, apart from enforcement</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The point deserves careful statement. Where parties compromise a suit and the court passes a decree in terms of the compromise, a party who breaks a term has broken a bargain, and the other side's remedy is ordinarily to execute the decree. That breach, without more, is not contempt. It is only where a party has given an undertaking to the court — an assurance addressed to the court and recorded as such — that breach invokes the contempt jurisdiction. The same disposal can contain both species: parties frequently agree terms between themselves and, in addition, specifically undertake to the court to perform particular obligations. Whether a given clause is one or the other turns on the language used and the manner in which the court recorded it.</p>

<h2>Undertakings in Delhi practice</h2>
<p>Certain undertakings appear constantly in Delhi litigation. A tenant or occupant undertakes to vacate premises by a fixed date, often in exchange for time; a judgment-debtor undertakes to pay in instalments against a stay of coercive steps; a defendant undertakes not to alienate, encumber or part with possession of property pending the suit; an accused or a party undertakes to appear on subsequent dates. In each of these, the court gives up something — time, an injunction, immediate execution — in reliance on the promise. That reliance is exactly why breach is treated as a wrong against the administration of justice and not merely against the opposite party.</p>

<h2>Wilfulness: the touchstone</h2>
<p>Section 2(b) punishes only <em>wilful</em> breach. Wilfulness implies deliberate, conscious disregard of the obligation — conduct that is intentional and with knowledge of the undertaking, not accidental, and not the product of genuine incapacity. Contempt proceedings are quasi-criminal in nature, so the breach and its wilfulness must be established clearly, and a benefit of genuine doubt goes to the respondent.</p>
<p>Recognised defences flow from this. Impossibility of performance arising after the undertaking, for reasons outside the party's control, negates wilfulness, though it must be promptly and candidly brought to the court's notice rather than pleaded after the default is discovered. Ambiguity in the undertaking is construed in favour of the alleged contemnor: a person cannot be punished for breaching an obligation whose scope was never clearly fixed. And a bona fide, reasonable understanding of what the undertaking required, even if ultimately wrong, undercuts the deliberateness that the section demands.</p>

<h2>Purging contempt</h2>
<p>Civil contempt is remedial at its core: the object is compliance, not retribution. A contemnor who performs the undertaking, even belatedly, and tenders a genuine apology is ordinarily said to have purged the contempt, and Section 12 expressly contemplates discharge or remission on an apology made to the court's satisfaction. But purging is not a free pass. Compliance extracted only after a contempt notice, or an apology offered as a tactic, may mitigate punishment without erasing the finding, and the court retains discretion over both.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Drafting discipline prevents most contempt litigation in this field. An undertaking should be precise about the act to be performed, dated with an unambiguous outer limit, conditional where performance genuinely depends on an external event, and given by the person who actually controls performance. A vague undertaking protects no one: it invites breach, and then invites a dispute about whether there was a breach at all.</p></div>

<h2>The sum of it</h2>
<p>An undertaking is the court's trust reduced to a sentence. Given carefully, it unlocks indulgences that litigants need — time, stays, consolidated settlements. Broken wilfully, it exposes the maker personally to imprisonment and fine under Section 12, over and above the enforcement of the obligation itself. The working rules are simple: record it precisely, promise only what can be performed, disclose supervening difficulties immediately, and never treat an assurance given to a court as just another negotiable term.</p>
